WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.703
Hello everyone. This is lecture eighteen of Mathematical Statistics One: expected values

00:05.803 --> 00:11.639
for continuous random variables, and the uniform distribution. It covers Wackerly sections

00:11.739 --> 00:15.991
four point three and four point four. The video follows the slides

00:16.091 --> 00:19.553
on the course page, so keep them open if you like, and

00:19.653 --> 00:22.652
pause whenever you want to try a step yourself.

00:24.433 --> 00:30.774
Last time we opened chapter four with the distribution function, capital F of y,

00:30.874 --> 00:36.249
and the density, small f of y, which is its derivative. A density is

00:36.349 --> 00:43.414
a height, not a probability. Probabilities are areas: the probability that Y lies between

00:43.514 --> 00:45.910
a and b is F of b minus F of a.

00:46.000 --> 00:50.787
Chapter three went the same way: first the distribution, then the mean

00:50.887 --> 00:55.395
and the variance that summarise it. Today we take that second step

00:55.495 --> 01:01.120
for continuous variables. The rule is one substitution: sums become integrals, and

01:01.220 --> 01:03.913
p of y becomes f of y, d y. Then we meet

01:04.013 --> 01:08.731
the first named continuous model, the simplest there is: the uniform.

01:10.500 --> 01:14.832
Here is the question for today. A plant is waiting for a replacement

01:14.932 --> 01:19.461
turbine part. Delivery takes between one and five days, and any time in

01:19.561 --> 01:24.415
that range is equally likely. Lost output costs two thousand manat, plus one

01:24.515 --> 01:28.783
hundred and fifty manat times the square of the delay in days. What

01:28.883 --> 01:32.499
should the budget line say the part will cost on average?

01:32.566 --> 01:37.058
The tempting answer plugs in the average delay, three days: two thousand

01:37.158 --> 01:41.395
plus one hundred and fifty times nine, which is three thousand three

01:41.495 --> 01:45.157
hundred and fifty manat. By the end of this lecture we will

01:45.257 --> 01:48.793
see that this number is too low, and by exactly how much.

01:50.566 --> 01:56.035
Definition four point five. The expected value of a continuous random variable

01:56.135 --> 01:59.963
Y is the integral, over the whole real line, of y times

02:00.063 --> 02:03.318
f of y, d y, provided that the integral exists.

02:04.000 --> 02:09.425
Where does it come from? Cut the axis into thin slices. The probability that

02:09.525 --> 02:13.643
Y lands in one slice is about its area: the height f of y,

02:13.743 --> 02:18.514
times the width, delta y. So each slice behaves like one value of a

02:18.614 --> 02:24.040
discrete variable, and the mean is about the sum of value times probability.

02:24.133 --> 02:28.964
Now make the slices thinner and thinner. The sum turns into an integral. So

02:29.064 --> 02:32.120
f of y, d y plays the role that p of y played in

02:32.220 --> 02:37.643
chapter three, and the integral adds up value times probability over all the slices.

02:37.733 --> 02:41.535
One remark on the word exists. It means that the integral

02:41.635 --> 02:44.548
of the absolute value of y, times f of y, is

02:44.648 --> 02:48.108
finite. Every model in this course passes that test.

02:49.900 --> 02:55.145
Theorem four point four. If g of Y is any function of Y, then its expected

02:55.245 --> 02:59.551
value is the integral of g of y times f of y. You do not need

02:59.651 --> 03:03.958
the distribution of g of Y; you weight g by the density of Y.

03:04.033 --> 03:08.823
Theorem four point five says that expectation is linear. The expected value of

03:08.923 --> 03:13.713
a constant is that constant. A constant factor comes outside. And the expected

03:13.813 --> 03:16.785
value of a sum is the sum of the expected values.

03:16.866 --> 03:19.920
Take g to be Y minus mu, squared, expand it, and

03:20.020 --> 03:24.126
these two theorems give the shortcut we know from chapter three:

03:24.226 --> 03:27.346
the variance is E of Y squared, minus mu squared.

03:29.133 --> 03:33.445
A worked example; it is Wackerly's example four point six. On a

03:33.545 --> 03:38.067
winter evening a grid operator must buy reserve power for Y hours,

03:38.167 --> 03:42.339
where Y is between zero and two, with density three eighths y

03:42.439 --> 03:47.031
squared. The density rises, so long shortfalls are the likely ones.

03:47.100 --> 03:50.414
The mean is the integral from zero to two of y

03:50.514 --> 03:54.943
times three eighths y squared. That is three eighths, times y

03:55.043 --> 03:59.248
to the fourth over four, evaluated from zero to two: three

03:59.348 --> 04:02.960
eighths times four, which is one point five hours.

04:03.033 --> 04:05.753
For E of Y squared, the integrand is y squared

04:05.853 --> 04:09.308
times three eighths y squared. We get three eighths, times

04:09.408 --> 04:12.128
thirty-two over five, which is two point four.

04:12.200 --> 04:15.024
So the variance is two point four minus one point

04:15.124 --> 04:18.665
five squared, which is point one five. The standard deviation

04:18.765 --> 04:22.784
is about point three eight seven hours, roughly twenty-three minutes.

04:24.566 --> 04:27.162
On the slides we check this in R, in two

04:27.262 --> 04:31.879
ways. First, numerical integration returns the same three numbers: one

04:31.979 --> 04:35.046
point five, two point four, and point one five.

04:35.133 --> 04:40.140
Second, a simulation. Here the distribution function is y cubed over eight. Set

04:40.240 --> 04:43.955
it equal to u and solve for y: the quantile function is two

04:44.055 --> 04:48.092
times the cube root of u. Draw u uniformly between zero and one,

04:48.192 --> 04:51.777
push it through that function, and out comes a draw of Y.

04:51.866 --> 04:55.551
With one hundred thousand draws, the sample mean and the

04:55.651 --> 04:59.808
sample variance agree with the integrals to two decimal places.

04:59.908 --> 05:02.916
Keep this trick in mind. It is why the uniform

05:03.016 --> 05:07.309
distribution matters: every simulation starts from uniform draws.

05:09.100 --> 05:13.761
Now turn hours into money. Reserve power costs the operator a fixed

05:13.861 --> 05:18.309
fee of five hundred manat, plus eight hundred manat per hour. So

05:18.409 --> 05:22.217
the cost is C equals five hundred plus eight hundred Y.

05:22.300 --> 05:25.824
By theorem four point five we need no new integral. The

05:25.924 --> 05:30.041
expected cost is five hundred plus eight hundred times one point

05:30.141 --> 05:34.588
five: one thousand seven hundred manat. The variance is eight hundred

05:34.688 --> 05:38.937
squared times point one five, which is ninety-six thousand, so the

05:39.037 --> 05:42.627
standard deviation is about three hundred and ten manat.

05:42.700 --> 05:47.488
Notice two things. The constant five hundred shifts the mean and leaves the

05:47.588 --> 05:51.919
spread alone. And for a linear cost, the expected cost is simply the

05:52.019 --> 05:55.504
cost of the expected hours. Hold on to the word linear.

05:57.300 --> 06:02.395
Section four point four. A correspondent bank promises a large transfer between ten

06:02.495 --> 06:06.527
o'clock and ten thirty, with no part of the window favoured: it is

06:06.627 --> 06:10.158
as likely to land in the first five minutes as in the last

06:10.258 --> 06:14.039
five. Let Y be the minutes after ten. Its density is flat, and

06:14.139 --> 06:18.045
since the total area must be one, the height is one over thirty.

06:18.133 --> 06:23.090
Definition four point six makes this general. For theta one less than

06:23.190 --> 06:28.293
theta two, Y has a continuous uniform distribution on the interval from

06:28.393 --> 06:32.324
theta one to theta two if its density is one over theta

06:32.424 --> 06:36.501
two minus theta one on that interval, and zero elsewhere.

06:36.600 --> 06:39.800
And definition four point seven gives us a word. The

06:39.900 --> 06:43.100
constants that fix the form of a density, here theta

06:43.200 --> 06:45.956
one and theta two, are called its parameters.

06:47.733 --> 06:52.231
For a uniform, probability is length over length. The probability

06:52.331 --> 06:55.981
that the transfer arrives in the last five minutes is

06:56.081 --> 06:59.094
the area of a strip of width five and height

06:59.194 --> 07:02.914
one over thirty: five over thirty, which is one sixth.

07:03.000 --> 07:06.202
Slide that strip anywhere inside the window and its area

07:06.302 --> 07:10.212
does not change. Every five-minute stretch has the same probability,

07:10.312 --> 07:13.514
one sixth. In general, the probability of an interval is

07:13.614 --> 07:16.640
its length divided by the length of the whole window.

07:16.733 --> 07:19.600
This is also Wackerly's example four point seven: if

07:19.700 --> 07:23.194
exactly one Poisson arrival occurred in a thirty-minute period,

07:23.294 --> 07:25.305
its time is uniform over that period.

07:27.100 --> 07:31.292
Theorem four point six. If Y is uniform on theta one to theta

07:31.392 --> 07:35.584
two, then its mean is theta one plus theta two, over two, and

07:35.684 --> 07:40.087
its variance is theta two minus theta one, squared, over twelve.

07:40.166 --> 07:43.968
The proof of the mean is one integral. Integrate y over theta

07:44.068 --> 07:48.125
two minus theta one. That gives theta two squared minus theta one

07:48.225 --> 07:52.410
squared, over two times theta two minus theta one. The numerator is

07:52.510 --> 07:56.375
a difference of squares, so one factor cancels and we are left

07:56.475 --> 07:59.893
with the midpoint. A flat block balances at its centre.

08:00.466 --> 08:05.584
The variance is exercise four point forty-one, and here is the sketch. E

08:05.684 --> 08:10.295
of Y squared is theta one squared, plus theta one theta two, plus

08:10.395 --> 08:15.731
theta two squared, all over three. Subtract mu squared, and it collapses to

08:15.831 --> 08:20.877
theta two minus theta one, squared, over twelve. It depends only on the

08:20.977 --> 08:24.791
width of the interval, not on where the interval sits.

08:26.566 --> 08:30.845
Let's use it. In a municipal road resurfacing tender, the low bid

08:30.945 --> 08:35.560
Y is uniform between four hundred and twenty and five hundred thousand

08:35.660 --> 08:39.736
manat. The width is eighty, so the density is one over eighty.

08:39.833 --> 08:43.907
The probability that the bid is below four hundred and forty is

08:44.007 --> 08:48.411
twenty over eighty, point two five. The probability that it is above

08:48.511 --> 08:52.585
four hundred and ninety is ten over eighty, point one two five.

08:52.666 --> 08:55.537
The mean is the midpoint, four hundred and sixty. The

08:55.637 --> 08:59.012
variance is eighty squared over twelve, about five hundred and

08:59.112 --> 09:03.329
thirty-three, so the standard deviation is about twenty-three thousand manat.

09:03.400 --> 09:07.034
Now suppose we learn that the bid is above four hundred

09:07.134 --> 09:10.837
and forty. What is the probability that it is above four

09:10.937 --> 09:15.454
hundred and eighty? Divide: twenty over eighty by sixty over eighty,

09:15.554 --> 09:19.935
which is one third. Conditioning a uniform on a subinterval leaves

09:20.035 --> 09:24.145
it uniform on that subinterval: twenty of the remaining sixty.

09:25.933 --> 09:30.069
Another uniform. A supermarket chain rounds each cash bill to the

09:30.169 --> 09:34.500
nearest five qapik. The rounding error, in manat, is uniform between

09:34.600 --> 09:38.084
minus point zero two five and plus point zero two five.

09:38.166 --> 09:41.635
By theorem four point six the mean is zero, so rounding

09:41.735 --> 09:45.659
is unbiased: the chain neither gains nor loses on average. The

09:45.759 --> 09:49.618
standard deviation is point zero five over the square root of

09:49.718 --> 09:52.538
twelve, about point zero one four four manat.

09:52.633 --> 09:56.455
How large is a typical error? Use theorem four point four with g

09:56.555 --> 10:00.745
equal to the absolute value. By symmetry it is twice the integral from

10:00.845 --> 10:04.483
zero to point zero two five, and the answer is point zero one

10:04.583 --> 10:08.160
two five manat. Mean zero is not size zero: each bill is off

10:08.260 --> 10:11.837
by one and a quarter qapik on average, one way or the other.

10:13.633 --> 10:17.203
Back to the delayed turbine part. The delay Y is uniform on

10:17.303 --> 10:20.625
one to five days, and the cost is two thousand plus one

10:20.725 --> 10:25.601
hundred and fifty Y squared. Compare Wackerly's exercise four point forty-seven.

10:25.700 --> 10:29.542
The mean delay is three days, and the variance is four squared over

10:29.642 --> 10:33.249
twelve, which is four thirds. So E of Y squared is the variance

10:33.349 --> 10:37.250
plus the mean squared: four thirds plus nine, thirty-one over three.

10:37.333 --> 10:40.062
By theorem four point five, the expected cost is

10:40.162 --> 10:43.362
two thousand plus one hundred and fifty times thirty-one

10:43.462 --> 10:46.663
over three: three thousand five hundred and fifty manat.

10:46.733 --> 10:51.821
Plugging in the average delay gave three thousand three hundred and fifty.

10:51.921 --> 10:55.818
The gap is one hundred and fifty times the variance of Y:

10:55.918 --> 11:00.164
two hundred manat. When g is not linear, the expected value of

11:00.264 --> 11:02.829
g of Y is not g of the expected value.

11:04.600 --> 11:08.618
Here is why the plug-in number is too low. Draw the cost against

11:08.718 --> 11:13.122
the delay. The curve bends upward: it is convex. At the average delay,

11:13.222 --> 11:17.691
three days, the curve stands at three thousand three hundred and fifty.

11:17.766 --> 11:21.434
Now take two equally likely delays, the same distance either side

11:21.534 --> 11:24.738
of three. Their average cost is the midpoint of the chord

11:24.838 --> 11:27.868
joining them, and a chord of a convex curve lies above

11:27.968 --> 11:31.694
the curve. The further apart the delays, the higher that midpoint.

11:31.766 --> 11:35.193
Average over all such pairs and you land at three thousand

11:35.293 --> 11:39.874
five hundred and fifty, above the curve. Long delays cost disproportionately,

11:39.974 --> 11:43.158
and the mean of Y squared remembers them. For a convex

11:43.258 --> 11:45.772
cost, the plug-in answer is always too low.

11:47.566 --> 11:50.618
Now it is your turn. In class this is the think,

11:50.718 --> 11:54.821
pair, share exercise. A crude tanker's loading time at a Caspian

11:54.921 --> 11:59.615
terminal is uniform between eighteen and thirty hours. The charterer pays

11:59.715 --> 12:03.949
demurrage of two thousand manat per hour beyond twenty-four hours.

12:04.033 --> 12:08.014
Three questions. One: find the mean and the variance of Y. Two:

12:08.114 --> 12:13.002
loading has already passed twenty-four hours; what is the probability that it

12:13.102 --> 12:17.925
goes beyond twenty-seven? Three: what is the expected demurrage bill, and is

12:18.025 --> 12:21.552
it what you get by plugging in the mean? Pause the video

12:21.652 --> 12:24.726
now, take about four minutes, and then come back.

12:27.800 --> 12:30.751
Here is the solution. By theorem four point six, the

12:30.851 --> 12:34.332
mean is eighteen plus thirty over two, twenty-four hours. The

12:34.432 --> 12:37.794
variance is twelve squared over twelve, which is twelve, so

12:37.894 --> 12:41.257
the standard deviation is about three point four six hours.

12:41.333 --> 12:44.235
Given that Y is above twenty-four, Y is uniform

12:44.335 --> 12:48.195
between twenty-four and thirty. The part above twenty-seven is

12:48.295 --> 12:51.708
three of the remaining six hours: probability one half.

12:51.800 --> 12:55.671
Demurrage is a hinge: zero up to twenty-four hours, then two

12:55.771 --> 12:59.841
thousand manat for each extra hour. By theorem four point four,

12:59.941 --> 13:04.673
integrate two thousand times y minus twenty-four, times one twelfth, from

13:04.773 --> 13:08.313
twenty-four to thirty. That gives three thousand manat.

13:08.400 --> 13:12.662
The plug-in answer is two thousand times zero, which is zero.

13:12.762 --> 13:16.237
A contract that charges only on the bad side has a

13:16.337 --> 13:21.100
positive expected cost, even when the average outcome costs nothing.

13:22.900 --> 13:26.509
A case study with real data. Take the daily close of the

13:26.609 --> 13:31.080
S and P five hundred from FRED, September twenty sixteen to September

13:31.180 --> 13:35.850
twenty twenty-six. In each calendar month, find the day with the largest

13:35.950 --> 13:40.488
absolute log return, and record its position in the month, rescaled to

13:40.588 --> 13:43.933
lie between zero and one. Call that Y. Is Y uniform?

13:44.000 --> 13:47.473
If it were uniform on zero to one, theorem four point six predicts a

13:47.573 --> 13:51.152
mean of one half and a variance of one twelfth, about point zero eight

13:51.252 --> 13:55.041
three. In one hundred and twenty months, the sample mean is point four six

13:55.141 --> 13:58.667
and the sample variance is point zero nine nine. The mean is close to

13:58.767 --> 14:01.767
one half, and the variance runs a little above one twelfth.

14:01.866 --> 14:06.008
The histogram sits reasonably near the flat line, but not everywhere. In

14:06.108 --> 14:09.426
the first quarter of the month the bars stand at one point

14:09.526 --> 14:13.727
four: thirty-five percent of the worst days fall there, where the uniform

14:13.827 --> 14:18.264
predicts twenty-five. Jobs and inflation releases come early in the month, so

14:18.364 --> 14:21.799
real news clusters near the start. The last bar is high too,

14:21.899 --> 14:25.806
and with only one hundred and twenty months some wobble is expected.

14:25.906 --> 14:29.459
The uniform is a sensible first model, but the position is not

14:29.559 --> 14:31.049
quite free of the calendar.

14:32.833 --> 14:37.149
Three quick questions to check yourself. First. A payment arrives at a

14:37.249 --> 14:42.196
uniformly distributed time within a forty-minute window. What is the variance of

14:42.296 --> 14:45.350
Y, in minutes squared? Pause if you want to think.

14:47.933 --> 14:50.961
It is forty squared over twelve, about one hundred

14:51.061 --> 14:54.464
and thirty-three. The variance is the squared width over

14:54.564 --> 14:57.341
twelve. Where the window sits does not matter.

14:57.433 --> 15:01.643
Second. Y is uniform on zero to two. What is E of Y squared?

15:04.233 --> 15:08.815
Four thirds, not one. By theorem four point four, integrate

15:08.915 --> 15:12.625
y squared times one half from zero to two: eight

15:12.725 --> 15:16.672
sixths. Or add the variance, one third, to the mean

15:16.772 --> 15:20.799
squared, one. Squaring the mean misses the variance.

15:20.866 --> 15:23.661
Third. A sealed bid is uniform between ten and

15:23.761 --> 15:27.375
fourteen thousand manat. Given that it exceeds eleven, what

15:27.475 --> 15:30.144
is the probability that it exceeds thirteen?

15:32.733 --> 15:35.329
One third. Given that Y is above eleven, it

15:35.429 --> 15:38.589
is uniform between eleven and fourteen, and the part

15:38.689 --> 15:41.723
above thirteen is one unit of the remaining three.

15:43.500 --> 15:47.724
Here are the key formulas on one page. The definition of the

15:47.824 --> 15:52.626
mean, and theorem four point four for any function g. Linearity, and

15:52.726 --> 15:56.662
the variance shortcut. For a linear cost a Y plus b, the

15:56.762 --> 16:00.194
mean is a mu plus b and the variance is a squared

16:00.294 --> 16:04.807
times the variance of Y. And for the uniform: flat density, mean

16:04.907 --> 16:08.555
at the midpoint, variance width squared over twelve.

16:10.333 --> 16:15.058
To sum up. Continuous expectation is chapter three with the sum replaced

16:15.158 --> 16:17.671
by an integral, and p of y by f of y, d

16:17.771 --> 16:21.960
y. Theorem four point four prices any function of Y, and theorem

16:22.060 --> 16:26.450
four point five handles linear ones with no new integral. A uniform

16:26.550 --> 16:32.012
gives probability proportional to length, and conditioned on a subinterval it stays

16:32.112 --> 16:36.233
uniform. Its mean is the midpoint and its variance is the width

16:36.333 --> 16:40.187
squared over twelve. And for a cost that is not linear, the

16:40.287 --> 16:43.940
expected cost is not the cost at the mean: a convex cost

16:44.040 --> 16:47.692
or a one-sided penalty makes the plug-in answer too low.

16:49.466 --> 16:53.021
For practice, from Wackerly, seventh edition. In section four

16:53.121 --> 16:56.257
point three, exercises four point twenty to four point

16:56.357 --> 17:00.212
thirty-three; start with four point twenty, twenty-six, thirty and

17:00.312 --> 17:03.807
thirty-two. In section four point four, exercises four point

17:03.907 --> 17:07.462
thirty-eight to four point fifty-three; start with four point

17:07.562 --> 17:10.578
forty-one, forty-five to forty-seven, and fifty-one.

17:10.666 --> 17:14.435
Also redo the delayed part with delivery uniform on two to

17:14.535 --> 17:18.370
four days: the same mean, a smaller spread. What happens to

17:18.470 --> 17:22.172
the two hundred manat gap? And week nine, problem set two

17:22.272 --> 17:26.107
is open on WeBWorK. It covers these two sections and closes

17:26.207 --> 17:30.576
on Sunday the fifteenth of November, at eleven fifty-nine at night.

17:32.366 --> 17:35.712
Next we turn to the most important continuous model of

17:35.812 --> 17:39.922
all: the normal distribution, Wackerly section four point five. If

17:40.022 --> 17:43.623
anything in this lecture is unclear, come to office hours,

17:43.723 --> 17:46.877
on Wednesdays from four to six in the afternoon, in

17:46.977 --> 17:50.003
room D three twenty-five. Thank you for watching.
